**C1-A. The csproj edit is comment-only (the escalation proof; YOUR program, not the builder's).** Parse the base and head blobs as XML (a comment-preserving parser such as `xml.etree.ElementTree.XMLParser(target=TreeBuilder(insert_comments=True))`, or a scanner you write): (1) the text with every `<!-- ... -->` removed is byte-equal between main and the head; (2) the one changed line lies wholly inside the comment block that opens at base line 31 and closes at base line 34; (3) no comment block holds an interior `--`; (4) `/usr/bin/grep -c -F 'Version="10.0.14"'` is 1 at both and `PreferPinnedPkcsOverSharedFramework`, `PackageConflictPreferredPackages`, `PackageReference` counts are equal at both. **Controls that can fail (scratch copies):** the pin changed to `10.0.15` is flagged; a copy with the comment closed early (`-->` inserted before the last words of the changed line) so a word falls outside is flagged; an unclosed comment fails the parse; a planted second changed line outside any comment is flagged. **Any byte outside a comment is a STOP for tier 1** (`STOPPED: NEEDS TUESDAY <path:line>`). **Is the new sentence TRUE?** Read the comment whole with the target below it and the pin line above it: the sentence says that when the SDK's targeting pack carries the same Pkcs version as the PackageReference (`(SDK 10.0.403 = 10.0.14)`), the tie goes to the package ranked first in `PackageConflictPreferredPackages`, which is the framework. The mechanism part is the earlier gate's measured row (RELAYED: `Briefs/2026-10-26_B722_STATUS.md` line 93, deleting the ranking line at 10.0.14 drops the DLL with "Choosing 'Platform:...' because it comes from a package that is preferred"); the example part `10.0.403 = 10.0.14` is RELAYED from `Briefs/2026-10-26_B722_evidence/c3-filever.txt` (the 10.0.403 install's framework Pkcs file version equals the 10.0.14 package's). **Your local SDK is 10.0.401 and cannot reproduce the tie: say so, mark the example RELAYED / NOT TESTED, never "verified".** You may read the evidence file as a claim and quote its two version strings. A comment that claims a mechanism the build does not have is a Minor finding; an example you can show false from the repo's own files (for example the pin line no longer reads 10.0.14 at the head) is a Major (class A, a comment that says what the build does not).
**C2. Branch 2: the assertions can fail, are first, and are stronger (the heart of branch 2).** FAIL: a replaced line that no mutant makes red; a replaced line that is red but never FIRST under any mutant you make; the new line weaker than the old one; the dropped guard's loss shown to matter; a sentence assertion changed; a row that passes only because its expectation is read from the code under test. **Property first:** *for each of the four rows (R3 = the row whose replaced line is base line 255, R4 = 271, R5 = 293, R6 = 309), there is a one-edit mutant of `ParityProof.cs` that makes the row's refusal disappear while the store is still non-agreeing, and under it the new line is the FIRST failing assertion of the row; and no store makes the old line fail while the new line passes (the new line implies the old: `AllAgree` is defined as `!Refused && ...`, read ParityProof.cs lines 34-36 and say so with the line numbers).* **Closed list as a property, then "at least these", then "any other shape: prove it meets the property or STOP":** write and run YOUR OWN mutants of `ParityProof.cs`, one anchor each (count exactly 1 printed with 1 line of context, sha256 before and after, tamper read back, build 0 warnings; a mutant that does not compile or warns is BROKEN: re-write it): **m-never** `if (!owed.SequenceEqual(have))` (line 199) -> `if (owed.Count < 0)` (the refusal never fires; do NOT use a constant `false`: it warns and the build is warnings-as-errors); **m-lt** -> `if (owed.Count < have.Count)` (fires only when the store holds MORE records than owed); **m-gt** -> `if (owed.Count > have.Count)` (fires only when it holds FEWER); **m-sentence** `int expected = owed.Count;` -> `int expected = owed.Count + 1;` (the refusal stays, the sentence changes); **m-dbl** the line-203 `sheets.Add(...)` written twice (a sheet listed twice); **m-cnt** line 203 `mine.Count` -> `mine.Count + 1` (the excluded-record count off by one). **Predictions first (the drafter's hypotheses from the source and the builder's relayed table, yours to confirm or contradict, never copied):** m-never: the new line is first red in R3, R4, R5, R6; m-lt: first red at the new line in all four (the four tampers keep the count equal or fewer than owed); m-gt: R3 and R6 (equal counts) first red at the new line, R4 and R5 GREEN at the new line and red only at the sentence line if the sentence differs, otherwise the row survives the mutant (record it: the builder names this a survivor class); m-sentence: the four new lines green, the four sentence lines red; m-dbl: only the row asserting the sheet list (the kept line at base 229) red; m-cnt: only the kept line at base 230 red. **Pass A (the base file, `wt-b775-b-mut` holding main's test file):** the same mutants; the four OLD lines are green under m-never (the old line cannot fail on these stores, because the synthetic workbook lacks the held option so `AllAgree` is false whatever the panel check answers) and the row dies only at the sentence line: this reproduces the earlier gate's finding and is the control that your instrument can see the blind spot (at least 3 of the 4 old lines must read GREEN at m-never in the base file, else your instrument cannot see the old blind spot and the pass is NOT EVIDENCE). **Pass B (the head file):** the same mutants, red rows BY NAME with the first failing assertion line (read the trx stack line, not the console alone), the frame (the new line, the sentence, or an earlier guard), compared to the builder's claim in a table (the builder's beside yours; a disagreement is a Note, a claimed-first line that is not first at your mutant is a Major of class B, M1: a row that cannot fail where it claims to). **Controls (in the head copy):** c0 unmutated (the parity class, 10 tests, green), c1 trailing comment, c2 whitespace-equivalent edit, c-fail (m-never must turn at least 1 row red, rc 1), **cw-weak** (a scratch copy of ONE row with the new line removed: green at c0; under m-never its first failure moves from the new line to the sentence line; print both). **Weak-assertion check by reading:** print the four new lines and say each is `Assert.True(report.Refused)` and what it catches that the sentence does not (a refusal that exists but with the wrong words is the sentence's job; a missing refusal is this line's). **The dropped guard (base line 290, `Assert.Equal(4, A4PanelRecords(doc))`):** prove or refute by YOUR probe that it read the same before and after its own tamper (the tamper removes a lower-case record, the helper counts upper-case SKUs); the line above it (base 289, `Assert.Equal(0, Held(doc, "SYN-C1", "pnl-a4-two"))`) proves the tamper landed: a mutant of the TEST's tamper (the `Where` predicate no longer matches) must turn the row red at line 289 or earlier. The builder discloses that the dropped guard WOULD be first under an over-reaching tamper (a fixture edit); judge it: dropped as the ticket's recommendation says = Note (state it as a candidate decision for Tuesday: keep or drop). **Exact-sentence lines:** the 9 `Assert.Equal("'SYN...` sentence lines and the `RESULT: REFUSED` rows are byte-equal at main and head, compared as ordered lists by program (print the count you find; the builder says 12; control: an altered line flagged). **Older kills retained:** the older `Assert.False(report.AllAgree)` lines (base 103, 122, 143, 210, 228) and `Assert.True(report.AllAgree, ...)` (84) are untouched; run the class under m-gt and m-lt and print which old lines go red first (information; edit nothing). **Whole Import project (193) under m-never, m-lt and m-gt** (cheap): the red set is not smaller than the parity class's (nothing outside this file's rows depends on the removed lines).
