## SEVERITY / VERDICT (the grading rule is decided HERE; apply it, do not re-argue it)
*Blocker (per branch):* a path beyond the branch's one path; a byte outside an XML comment in the csproj, or a changed pin line (also a STOP for tier 1); a removed or edited existing line of the test file other than the five listed; a sentence assertion changed or loosened; a non-ASCII byte, a person's name, a decision-card id, a ticket key or `B###` token in an added line or a message (other than the carried-over comment text); the branch's head moved; a textual conflict; a secret; a CodeQL alert open on a head; a failing test at the head (after the file is re-run alone twice, and the red NOT reproduced at main); a build warning (the build is warnings-as-errors); a product byte changed.
*Major, class SAME:* **(M1) a replaced line of branch 2 that cannot fail**, or that no mutant of yours makes red, or whose claimed-first line is not first at your mutant; **(M2) an assertion weakened** (a new line that passes where the old one failed). *Major, class DIFFERENT:* a builder claim contradicted by measurement with a verdict consequence (1 commit, 1 path, +1 -1 / +4 -5, comment-only by program, names lost 0 added 0, 435/193/3063 within drift you explain, publish output identical, the dropped guard "never first" if your probe shows a mutant of the PRODUCT that is first there and no other line catches); a comment sentence you can show false from the repo's own files; a path shared with a live lane or an open PR.
*Minor / Note (never a Major):* the survivor class "a refusal that fires only on FEWER records" if it survives the new lines of R4 and R5 but is killed by older rows (row-strength note; name the killing row); the dropped guard if it is judged a loss only under an over-reaching tamper (a candidate decision: keep or drop); the example `10.0.403 = 10.0.14` RELAYED and not reproducible on SDK 10.0.401 (UNMEASURED-ON-SDK-10.0.403); `B160` / `B156` / `B98` carried in the csproj comment; unsigned commits; no PR yet so no CI to read; the neighbouring capped class and the case-folding and sentence-only survivors (other tickets, other lanes: name them, never a finding against this branch); any survivor of the strip-one-character family (not in scope). *Where the line between Note and Major is unclear:* a thing that makes a new line unable to fail, or a comment that claims what the build does not do, is at least Major; a thing that only narrows how strong a line is, stays true to the commission and is disclosed, is a Note. Say which rule you applied for every finding. **Round 1 of 2 per class: do not pad a Note into a NO GO and do not soften a Blocker: quote the plant, the row and the number.**
**VERDICT RULES.** GO needs the branch's checks clean and measured, every named mutant red as claimed, 0 findings above Note, and its CI predicate read. With no PR there is no CodeQL scan and no hosted `dotnet` check: **the best token for each branch is GO WITH FOLLOW-UPS (conditional: CodeQL NOT SCANNED, the hosted `dotnet` job not run; Tuesday reads them on the PR)** unless the PR already exists and is read. GO WITH FOLLOW-UPS = Minor/Note/Condition only. NO GO on any Blocker or Major of that branch. NOT GATED = that head moved, a moved guarded region, or a returned READY. A security-shaped finding or a csproj byte outside a comment is a STOP for tier 1.
